        "Functions: substitute carefully, working from inside out",
        "Iteration: keep substituting until values converge (or as instructed)"
      ],
      "title": "Quadratic nth Term, Functions & Iteration",
      "content": "<p><strong>Quadratic sequences</strong> have a second difference that is constant. The nth term has the form \\(an^2 + bn + c\\).</p><p>To find \\(a\\): \\(2a\\) = second difference, so \\(a = \\frac{\\text{second difference}}{2}\\). Then subtract \\(an^2\\) from each term to get a linear sequence, and find \\(bn + c\\) as normal.</p><p><strong>Functions:</strong> \\(f(x)\\) notation means substitute \\(x\\) into the function. Composite functions: \\(fg(x)\\) means apply \\(g\\) first, then \\(f\\). Inverse \\(f^{-1}(x)\\): swap \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), then rearrange.</p><p><strong>Iteration:</strong> A formula \\(x_{n+1} = g(x_n)\\) generates a sequence. Start with \\(x_0\\) (or \\(x_1\\)), substitute repeatedly. The sequence may converge to a root.</p>",
      "example": "<p><strong>Find the nth term of</strong> 3, 8, 15, 24, 35, ...</p><p><strong>1st differences:</strong> 5, 7, 9, 11</p><p><strong>2nd difference:</strong> 2 → \\(a = 1\\)</p><p><strong>Subtract \\(n^2\\):</strong> 3−1, 8−4, 15−9, 24−16 = 2, 4, 6, 8 → \\(2n\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(n^2 + 2n\\)</p>"
    },
